A direct scan is enough here. I check every value and increase the answer whenever it matches `target`. The sorted order is not needed for this version, which makes the code easy to follow.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countOccurrences(vector<int>& arr, int target) {
        int count = 0;

        for (int value : arr) {
            if (value == target) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The recursive function searches a sorted range. If the middle value is smaller or larger than the target, only one half is needed. When the middle matches, both halves are checked so every matching position is counted.

An empty array or a target that is not present returns `0` naturally.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countOccurrences(vector<int>& arr, int target) {
        return countInRange(arr, target, 0, static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1);
    }

private:
    int countInRange(vector<int>& arr, int target, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return 0;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[middle] < target) {
            return countInRange(arr, target, middle + 1, right);
        }

        if (arr[middle] > target) {
            return countInRange(arr, target, left, middle - 1);
        }

        return 1
            + countInRange(arr, target, left, middle - 1)
            + countInRange(arr, target, middle + 1, right);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case when many values match  
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case because of recursive calls

This version keeps a frequency table while scanning the array. Every matching value adds one to the stored count, and the final value is returned.

The array being sorted is not used by this method. That is the trade-off: it is very clear, but it does not get the faster binary search time.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int countOccurrences(int[] arr, int target) {
        var frequencies = new System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary<int, int>();

        foreach (int value in arr) {
            if (frequencies.ContainsKey(value)) {
                frequencies[value]++;
            } else {
                frequencies[value] = 1;
            }
        }

        if (frequencies.ContainsKey(target)) {
            return frequencies[target];
        }

        return 0;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` average  
Space complexity: `O(u)`, where `u` is the number of different values

The first binary search finds the first position where `target` can appear. The second finds the first position after all copies of `target`. Their difference gives the count.

For example, in `[1, 2, 2, 2, 5]`, the two positions are `1` and `4`, so the answer is `4 - 1 = 3`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countOccurrences(int[] arr, int target) {
        int first = lowerBound(arr, target);
        int afterLast = upperBound(arr, target);

        return afterLast - first;
    }

    private int lowerBound(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[middle] < target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }

    private int upperBound(int[] arr, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[middle] <= target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

I first locate one occurrence with binary search. Since equal values are next to each other in a sorted array, I then move left and right to count the complete block.

Dry run for `[1, 3, 3, 3, 7]` and target `3`: binary search can stop at the middle `3`. Moving left finds the block start at index `1`, and moving right ends at index `3`. The count is `3`.

This is simple, but if the target fills most of the array, the expansion takes linear time.

```python
class Solution:
    def countOccurrences(self, arr, target):
        left = 0
        right = len(arr) - 1
        found = -1

        while left <= right:
            middle = left + (right - left) // 2

            if arr[middle] == target:
                found = middle
                break
            if arr[middle] < target:
                left = middle + 1
            else:
                right = middle - 1

        if found == -1:
            return 0

        start = found
        while start > 0 and arr[start - 1] == target:
            start -= 1

        end = found
        while end + 1 < len(arr) and arr[end + 1] == target:
            end += 1

        return end - start + 1
```

Time complexity: `O(log n + k)`, where `k` is the number of matches  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

